# ShellCheck configuration.
#
# This file exists so that the three places shell gets linted — the pre-commit
# hook, `make lint`, and the CI job — cannot silently disagree about what
# "clean" means. None of them passes check-selection flags; they all read this.
#
# A linter whose local and CI configurations differ is worse than no linter,
# because a developer whose commit was green then fails in CI learns to
# distrust the local one and stops running it.

# Resolve `source`d files relative to the sourcing script, not the caller's
# working directory. Nothing sources anything today; this is here so that the
# first script which does is not reported as an unresolvable source.
source-path=SCRIPTDIR

# Everything at severity `style` and above. This is ShellCheck's own default,
# written out because a default that is relied upon should be visible.
severity=style

# Optional checks, off by default upstream, enabled here. Each one catches a
# class of bug rather than a matter of taste, and each is at zero findings
# across the repository today — so they enforce the current state rather than
# leaving a backlog behind them.
#
#   SC2244  [ "$x" ]  is true for the string "0" and false for the empty
#           string. It reads like a boolean test and is not one; -n says it.
#   SC2154  a referenced-but-never-assigned variable, which is nearly always
#           a typo in the name. `set -u` catches it at runtime, on the branch
#           that happens to be taken; this catches it before that.
#   SC2230  `which` is an external command with inconsistent exit behaviour
#           across systems. `command -v` is a builtin and specified by POSIX.
#   SC2248  unquoted expansion, even where the value looks safe today. The
#           value stops being safe when someone changes what fills it.
enable=avoid-nullary-conditions
enable=check-unassigned-uppercase
enable=deprecate-which
enable=quote-safe-variables

# Deliberately NOT enabled, recorded so the omission reads as a decision:
#
#   check-extra-masked-returns (SC2312)  4 findings. Under `set -euo pipefail`
#           a failing command inside `$( )` does not stop the script, so its
#           empty output is consumed as if it were an answer. That is exactly
#           the failure class this repository is built around, and one of the
#           four is real: preflight.sh builds a SQL admin password from
#           `$(openssl rand ...)`, and if openssl is absent the create fails
#           and the script reports Azure SQL as RESTRICTED in the region.
#           ARCHITECTURE.md §6a moved the whole platform on that signal.
#           Enabling this check is a script change, not a lint change, so it
#           is tracked separately rather than turned on with suppressions.
#
#   add-default-case (SC2249)  1 finding, and it is a false one: the
#           spending-limit case warns on `On` and correctly says nothing for
#           `Off`. A default branch there would be noise.
#
#   require-double-brackets (SC2292), require-variable-braces (SC2250)
#           23 and 89 findings, all matters of taste. Enforcing a preference
#           at the same severity as a bug teaches people to skim the output,
#           and the whole value of this linter is that its output gets read.
